WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:02.901
-Our next guest is an Academy
Award-nominated director.

00:00:02.901 --> 00:00:04.701
His film "Postcard from Earth"

00:00:04.701 --> 00:00:07.400
is showing exclusively
at The Sphere in Las Vegas.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:09.767
Please welcome to the show
Darren Aronofsky.

00:00:09.767 --> 00:00:12.400
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:22.400
♪♪

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:23.868
♪♪

00:00:23.868 --> 00:00:26.234
Thank you for being here.
-Thanks for having me.

00:00:26.234 --> 00:00:29.901
I'm very excited to talk about
this technical marvel of a film,

00:00:29.901 --> 00:00:31.300
but I also want to start.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.801
The first time I became aware
of you was your film "Pi,"

00:00:33.801 --> 00:00:36.167
which came out in the late '90s,
which is a wonderful film.

00:00:36.167 --> 00:00:37.367
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:37.367 --> 00:00:39.234
We just had
our 25th anniversary.

00:00:39.234 --> 00:00:40.934
-Just had the 25th.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:00:40.934 --> 00:00:43.133
-You made this movie
for $60,000?

00:00:43.133 --> 00:00:44.167
-Yeah. Pretty much.

00:00:44.167 --> 00:00:47.834
-And so it was
a very lo-fi film.

00:00:47.834 --> 00:00:49.501
It was just, like, cut and run.
You made it.

00:00:49.501 --> 00:00:50.801
But it was great storytelling,
and it was

00:00:50.801 --> 00:00:53.400
the beginning of things.
And now this is so different,

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:54.767
what you did with this.
-How things change.

00:00:54.767 --> 00:00:55.868
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:55.868 --> 00:00:59.434
So, The Sphere is in Las Vegas.
-Yeah.

00:00:59.434 --> 00:01:01.501
Everyone's seen that thing.
-There you go.

00:01:01.501 --> 00:01:03.300
-Yeah.
-And so when they first

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.334
approached you, like, what did
you know about this sphere?

00:01:06.334 --> 00:01:09.067
-I knew it was a crazy idea.
-Yeah.

00:01:09.067 --> 00:01:11.601
-But I couldn't really imagine
what a TV screen

00:01:11.601 --> 00:01:14.601
four football fields large
would feel like.

00:01:14.601 --> 00:01:18.067
I actually didn't get to see it
till August of this year.

00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:20.133
That was the first time
they turned it on.

00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:22.667
And so we had been working
on these crazy images,

00:01:22.667 --> 00:01:24.968
and then suddenly,
to see it everywhere,

00:01:24.968 --> 00:01:26.501
was a wild journey.

00:01:26.501 --> 00:01:30.834
-So, you filmed this movie
not quite being able to conceive

00:01:30.834 --> 00:01:32.567
of what it would look
like when projected.

00:01:32.567 --> 00:01:33.901
-It was a lot of guesswork.

00:01:33.901 --> 00:01:35.567
It was kind of shooting blind.

00:01:35.567 --> 00:01:38.067
It was described
like flying the airplane

00:01:38.067 --> 00:01:39.801
while building it,
and it was very much that.

00:01:39.801 --> 00:01:41.934
'Cause the technology
didn't quite exist

00:01:41.934 --> 00:01:44.367
and how we were going
to actually interpret

00:01:44.367 --> 00:01:45.634
all that data.

00:01:45.634 --> 00:01:49.601
So, the camera outputs
32 gigs per second.

00:01:49.601 --> 00:01:52.367
So like a movie on your
hard drive is three gigs.

00:01:52.367 --> 00:01:55.067
So this is 30 gigs --
32 gigs per second

00:01:55.067 --> 00:01:56.400
to make an 18K image.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:58.400
So the amount of information
was just --

00:01:58.400 --> 00:01:59.667
it was kind of mind-blowing.

00:01:59.667 --> 00:02:01.501
-What was the size
of the camera?

00:02:01.501 --> 00:02:04.067
[ Laughter ]
-So -- exactly.

00:02:04.067 --> 00:02:06.300
It took about
12 people to turn this camera on

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:09.133
because there was, like, fans
and overheating.

00:02:09.133 --> 00:02:11.901
And then, the final file
of the film

00:02:11.901 --> 00:02:14.200
is what they call
a half a petabyte.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:16.000
-Okay.
-So that's after terabyte

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.501
is now a petabyte.

00:02:17.501 --> 00:02:19.133
I guess we'll all start...
-So, like, you

00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:21.300
probably had to, like,
use Maildrop to send it.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:22.667
-Exactly, exactly.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:22.667 --> 00:02:24.934
Re-transfer, please.

00:02:24.934 --> 00:02:27.868
Yeah.
-And so I also want to --

00:02:27.868 --> 00:02:30.067
So, the exterior of the --

00:02:30.067 --> 00:02:32.834
And again, I was seeing images
of The Sphere online,

00:02:32.834 --> 00:02:35.100
and I didn't quite comprehend
what I was seeing, but...

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.501
-Yeah.
So this is actually a 2K image.

00:02:37.501 --> 00:02:38.767
-Okay.
-So that's 2K.

00:02:38.767 --> 00:02:40.567
We're 18K on the inside.

00:02:40.567 --> 00:02:41.901
-All right.
So just to give a sense

00:02:41.901 --> 00:02:44.067
of what you can do with 2K...
Like, that's --

00:02:44.067 --> 00:02:45.334
I mean, if you see that
you realize --

00:02:45.334 --> 00:02:46.567
-That's a real image.
-I know.

00:02:46.567 --> 00:02:48.234
And that's, like,
if you're in Vegas,

00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:50.234
you have a late night in Vegas,
and you wake up...

00:02:50.234 --> 00:02:51.868
[ Laughter ]

00:02:51.868 --> 00:02:53.400
-It's true.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:56.634
This is Summer League.
That's crazy.

00:02:56.634 --> 00:02:58.367
That is, uh...
-Yeah, that's --

00:02:58.367 --> 00:03:00.868
that's a very popular one.
-That's a very popular one.

00:03:00.868 --> 00:03:03.667
And this is,
U2 is doing a concert there.

00:03:03.667 --> 00:03:05.901
And then, the other...
-Attraction.

00:03:05.901 --> 00:03:07.033
-Attraction.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:08.434
-Is your film.
-When U2's not there,

00:03:08.434 --> 00:03:10.734
we're there.
Basically the thing.

00:03:10.734 --> 00:03:12.501
-It's not the other way around?
-No, they got first dibs

00:03:12.501 --> 00:03:13.601
on everything.
-Okay, good.

00:03:13.601 --> 00:03:14.868
-It's good. All respect.

00:03:14.868 --> 00:03:16.100
-Yeah, yeah, very much so.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:19.901
But, so the first time
the audience saw 18K...

00:03:19.901 --> 00:03:21.434
-Yeah.
-...and somebody

00:03:21.434 --> 00:03:22.868
posted on Instagram.
-Oh, yeah.

00:03:22.868 --> 00:03:25.634
-Can you talk about
the moment as you, a director --

00:03:25.634 --> 00:03:27.901
You hadn't seen this technology
until recently, as well.

00:03:27.901 --> 00:03:29.467
What was it like?
-It was great.

00:03:29.467 --> 00:03:32.367
It was -- it was
what we were hoping for.

00:03:32.367 --> 00:03:35.534
So, no one had ever seen it,

00:03:35.534 --> 00:03:37.234
so we didn't really know
what they would respond.

00:03:37.234 --> 00:03:40.567
And then this moment happened,
and it was just, like...

00:03:40.567 --> 00:03:42.100
I don't know.
You always hope --

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:45.100
I actually didn't know that
jaw-dropping was a real thing.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:47.834
You hear that word, but it's fun
to look around,

00:03:47.834 --> 00:03:50.234
and people are just like --
their jaws are literally --

00:03:50.234 --> 00:03:51.834
literally dropped,
and they're all pointing

00:03:51.834 --> 00:03:53.434
at different places
on the screen.

00:03:53.434 --> 00:03:55.934
So it's a joy to be --

00:03:55.934 --> 00:03:58.200
to kind of have audiences
go along for that trip.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:00.067
-Let's just take a look
at this short video

00:04:00.067 --> 00:04:03.467
just to see the reaction
to the first time seeing 18K.

00:04:03.467 --> 00:04:09.000
♪♪

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.701
Whoa!

00:04:10.701 --> 00:04:13.467
[ Audience exclaims, applause ]

00:04:13.467 --> 00:04:16.033
-This is crazy.

00:04:16.033 --> 00:04:18.634
♪♪

00:04:18.634 --> 00:04:20.767
-Yeah.
-We have to stress, of course,

00:04:20.767 --> 00:04:23.167
that's on someone's phone.
-Yes, exactly.

00:04:23.167 --> 00:04:24.501
[ Laughter ]

00:04:24.501 --> 00:04:27.300
-That's not what it's gonna
look like for you guys.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.467
It's true. It's --
I was trying to shoot --

00:04:29.467 --> 00:04:32.634
I was lucky enough to post
the first images of it,

00:04:32.634 --> 00:04:34.968
and I just --
I was sitting in that room

00:04:34.968 --> 00:04:36.400
forever trying
to get a good image,

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:38.734
and not to diss iPhone,
but it just wasn't...

00:04:38.734 --> 00:04:41.634
-No. Well, I mean, I guess
that's why it is an attraction

00:04:41.634 --> 00:04:43.100
worth going to see,
because there's no way to see it

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:44.133
unless you're actually there
in person.

00:04:44.133 --> 00:04:45.801
-Yeah. And so --
and it's a strange thing

00:04:45.801 --> 00:04:47.667
to try to tell people
what it's like,

00:04:47.667 --> 00:04:49.934
but it's -- it's kind of
a nature film

00:04:49.934 --> 00:04:53.701
where we shot in every continent
on the planet --

00:04:53.701 --> 00:04:55.501
we went to the Antarctic,
we went to Australia,

00:04:55.501 --> 00:04:59.033
we went everywhere -- and just
shot just the coolest stuff

00:04:59.033 --> 00:05:00.901
that you could imagine

00:05:00.901 --> 00:05:04.267
but with a resolution
that's hard to sort of take in.

00:05:04.267 --> 00:05:06.501
And we also actually
shot micro images.

00:05:06.501 --> 00:05:09.501
We shot -- there's one shot
of a praying mantis.

00:05:09.501 --> 00:05:11.734
And most people, when you think
of a praying mantis,

00:05:11.734 --> 00:05:13.133
you're like with
a magnifying glass

00:05:13.133 --> 00:05:15.133
or you're really close,
staring at it.

00:05:15.133 --> 00:05:20.033
But this is a 150-foot tall
praying mantis, fully in focus.

00:05:20.033 --> 00:05:21.467
And so you could look at it --

00:05:21.467 --> 00:05:23.767
And so it's not like
anything you've seen before.

00:05:23.767 --> 00:05:25.734
It's pretty cool.
-Now, nature --

00:05:25.734 --> 00:05:26.934
You've actually done some

00:05:26.934 --> 00:05:28.434
sort of nature filming
before this, as well.

00:05:28.434 --> 00:05:31.100
Was that something -- when
you first got into filmmaking,

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:33.701
is that a path you thought
you would maybe take?

00:05:33.701 --> 00:05:35.634
-I come from that background.

00:05:35.634 --> 00:05:38.234
I was originally trained
as a field biologist.

00:05:38.234 --> 00:05:41.133
Strangely enough, I did a bunch
of research out in nature,

00:05:41.133 --> 00:05:42.501
and I've been kind of connected

00:05:42.501 --> 00:05:44.801
to lots of green groups
over the years.

00:05:44.801 --> 00:05:47.834
And then, a really
smart executive

00:05:47.834 --> 00:05:49.934
remembered that about my bio
and connected me

00:05:49.934 --> 00:05:51.334
with a show called
"One Strange Rock,"

00:05:51.334 --> 00:05:52.601
and it led to
"Welcome to Earth"

00:05:52.601 --> 00:05:55.033
and "Limitless"
and all this Nat Geo work I do.

00:05:55.033 --> 00:05:56.834
So, when this came along,
I was --

00:05:56.834 --> 00:05:59.567
I was able to get
the best people in the business

00:05:59.567 --> 00:06:01.400
to come work on it with us.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:03.367
-How did you find your way
into filmmaking?

00:06:03.367 --> 00:06:06.033
Was that something
that came to you in college?

00:06:06.033 --> 00:06:07.701
-Yeah.

00:06:07.701 --> 00:06:10.634
I went to college, and I
really didn't know how to write,

00:06:10.634 --> 00:06:14.667
and my roommate was making
animation cartoons,

00:06:14.667 --> 00:06:16.534
and he was getting A's,
and I was getting B's,

00:06:16.534 --> 00:06:18.133
and I was like,
"Oh, maybe I should start

00:06:18.133 --> 00:06:21.334
taking drawing classes."
So that's how it started.

00:06:21.334 --> 00:06:23.467
-And when you go out
in nature,

00:06:23.467 --> 00:06:25.734
especially, again,
when you have this camera

00:06:25.734 --> 00:06:26.868
that no one's ever used
before...

00:06:26.868 --> 00:06:28.434
-Yeah, yeah.

00:06:28.434 --> 00:06:30.801
-...I mean, are you just --
is it so thrilling to know

00:06:30.801 --> 00:06:32.133
that you're going to find
the things

00:06:32.133 --> 00:06:34.834
that you would want people
to see for the first time?

00:06:34.834 --> 00:06:36.334
-Yeah. It's hard.

00:06:36.334 --> 00:06:38.901
It was a very -- it was
kind of a jigsaw puzzle in --

00:06:38.901 --> 00:06:41.834
because the camera was
really unwieldy.

00:06:41.834 --> 00:06:43.100
It was very --
-I can't believe

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:45.334
the praying mantis, like,
stayed still for a shot.

00:06:45.334 --> 00:06:46.734
[ Laughter ]

00:06:46.734 --> 00:06:49.567
-You know, I think you had
to shoot things that were --

00:06:49.567 --> 00:06:52.601
that -- that held still, and,
like, a praying mantis,

00:06:52.601 --> 00:06:54.133
after it eats something
sort of sits there

00:06:54.133 --> 00:06:56.200
for a while, digesting,
so that's when we shot it.

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:57.267
[ Laughter ]

00:06:57.267 --> 00:06:58.868
But you look
for things like that.

00:06:58.868 --> 00:07:00.601
We tried to move the camera
a few times.

00:07:00.601 --> 00:07:01.801
It's very tricky.

00:07:01.801 --> 00:07:03.801
And then, we did a lot
of really cool stuff.

00:07:03.801 --> 00:07:06.234
We attached it to a --
to a helicopter

00:07:06.234 --> 00:07:09.067
and flew it around for a while,
which was -- which was great

00:07:09.067 --> 00:07:11.834
because I got to do that
with these incredible pilots.

00:07:11.834 --> 00:07:13.133
And we were up in Oregon
when there was

00:07:13.133 --> 00:07:15.167
all these incredible
forest fires,

00:07:15.167 --> 00:07:19.167
and we were just flying over
the most incredible landscape

00:07:19.167 --> 00:07:22.067
and just knowing
we were capturing it

00:07:22.067 --> 00:07:24.601
with something that looked as
real as what we were seeing.

00:07:24.601 --> 00:07:26.400
-So obviously, you love nature,

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:27.634
you love being in the outdoors.

00:07:27.634 --> 00:07:29.334
How did you enjoy your time
in Las Vegas?

00:07:29.334 --> 00:07:31.534
[ Laughter ]

00:07:31.534 --> 00:07:34.234
-There's a lot of -- a lot
of great talent comes through.

00:07:34.234 --> 00:07:37.067
I saw Adele twice,
so I can't -- I can't whine.

00:07:37.067 --> 00:07:40.834
It was an amazing show.
I saw Copperfield, I saw --

00:07:40.834 --> 00:07:42.868
I just tried to take
in all the different things.

00:07:42.868 --> 00:07:44.701
-How much time did you have
to actually

00:07:44.701 --> 00:07:47.601
technically spend
in Las Vegas?

00:07:47.601 --> 00:07:50.567
-Too much, but I think it was --
But there is --

00:07:50.567 --> 00:07:52.467
There's also the Pinball
Hall of Fame, which is --

00:07:52.467 --> 00:07:53.634
-Yeah. I have friends
who've gone

00:07:53.634 --> 00:07:54.667
to the Pinball Hall of Fame.
-It's amazing.

00:07:54.667 --> 00:07:56.234
-Do you have
a favorite pinball game?

00:07:56.234 --> 00:07:57.834
-Oh, that's a good question.

00:07:57.834 --> 00:07:59.667
It depends what era.
What?

00:07:59.667 --> 00:08:02.767
But the old school,
it was a game called "Taxi,"

00:08:02.767 --> 00:08:04.167
where you have to
pick up Gorbachev.

00:08:04.167 --> 00:08:07.334
-Yeah. Oh.
-Old school, you know.

00:08:07.334 --> 00:08:09.334
-He was in a --
-"Taxi" plus "Gorbachev" --

00:08:09.334 --> 00:08:11.467
I think we know when it's from.
-Exactly.

00:08:11.467 --> 00:08:13.400
And then --
But, you know,

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:15.434
I got into "The Twilight Zone"
game this last time.

00:08:15.434 --> 00:08:16.901
-"Medieval Madness"?
You ever play "Medieval..."

00:08:16.901 --> 00:08:17.934
-Love it, love it.
-That's a great game.

00:08:17.934 --> 00:08:19.334
-Great game, yeah.
-All right.

00:08:19.334 --> 00:08:21.634
I think when you tell people you
went to Vegas, and they're like,

00:08:21.634 --> 00:08:23.200
"What'd you do?"
you're like, "Pinball, baby,"

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:26.801
they're like, "Oh. You're the
wrong person to go to Vegas."

00:08:26.801 --> 00:08:29.033
-I did have a run, though,
after --

00:08:29.033 --> 00:08:32.734
So, like, after the premiere,
and people really liked it,

00:08:32.734 --> 00:08:34.334
and I was at the casino --
I don't gamble --

00:08:34.334 --> 00:08:36.100
and my friends
were at the table all losing,

00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:37.400
and I was like, "You know what?
All this, like,

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:39.167
positive energy is my way."

00:08:39.167 --> 00:08:40.267
And I did a run,
and I walked out

00:08:40.267 --> 00:08:41.868
with 800 bucks in my pocket.

00:08:41.868 --> 00:08:44.167
-800 bucks?
That's fantastic.

00:08:44.167 --> 00:08:46.734
-Boom!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:46.734 --> 00:08:49.801
-That's how many --
That's how crazy the pixels are.

00:08:49.801 --> 00:08:52.267
There's more pixels than there
are money you won in Vegas.

00:08:52.267 --> 00:08:54.067
-Exactly, exactly.
-Hey, thanks so much

00:08:54.067 --> 00:08:55.367
for being here.
I really can't wait to

00:08:55.367 --> 00:08:57.400
see it live.
Darren Aronofsky, everybody.

00:08:57.400 --> 00:08:59.300
"Postcard from Earth"
showing exclusive

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:00.567
at The Sphere in Las Vegas.

00:09:00.567 --> 00:09:01.801
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

